<section class="section" id="section-fabricspec">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section__header">
      <span class="section__label">Fabric Specifications</span>
      <div class="accent-line"></div>
      <h2 class="section__title">Pet Clothing Fabric Specs | What the Numbers Actually Mean</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="text-content">
      <p>"Waterproof" and "pet-safe" are marketing words until they come with a number attached. Here is exactly what we test, what the numbers mean for your product, and the minimum spec we work to on every pet apparel order.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Property</th>
            <th>What We Test</th>
            <th>SDF Minimum Spec</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Waterproofing (rain coats, outer shells)</td>
            <td>Water column rating in millimetres | the height of water a fabric resists before leaking</td>
            <td>3,000mm for light rain gear, 5,000mm+ for serious outdoor coats</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Insulation weight (fleece, thermal linings)</td>
            <td>GSM | grams per square metre of fabric</td>
            <td>250-300 GSM polar fleece for winter coats and hoodies</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Shrinkage after washing</td>
            <td>Dimensional stability across 3 wash cycles</td>
            <td>Maximum +/- 3% shrinkage | cheap cotton can shrink 10%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dye bleeding (colour safety)</td>
            <td>Colour fastness for rubbing and washing, graded 1-5</td>
            <td>Grade 4 minimum | prevents dye transfer onto light-coloured pets</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pilling resistance (knitwear, sweaters)</td>
            <td>Fabric surface wear after repeated friction</td>
            <td>Grade 3.5 minimum | stays smooth, not fuzzy, after regular wear</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stretch and recovery (necklines, leg openings)</td>
            <td>Spandex/elastane content in cotton and fleece blends</td>
            <td>3-5% spandex blend | stretches for movement, snaps back to shape</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Claw and tooth resistance (active wear, harness-compatible tops)</td>
            <td>Tear resistance under point load</td>
            <td>Ripstop nylon with reinforced grid weave on request</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    
    <p class="text-content" style="margin-top: 2rem;">Every fabric batch ships with a lab test report covering the specs relevant to that product. If a supplier cannot give you a water column rating in millimetres or a GSM number, they are guessing | not testing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="section-casestudy">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section__header">
      <span class="section__label">Case Study</span>
      <div class="accent-line"></div>
      <h2 class="section__title">From 18% Returns to Under 2% | A Sizing Fix Case Study</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="text-content">
      <p>A UK pet apparel brand came to us after a previous factory shipped a hoodie run that did not fit French Bulldogs. The chest was cut too narrow and the neck opening was too loose | a direct result of using a generic "small dog" block instead of a breed-specific pattern.</p>
      
      <p>Our pattern team reviewed the existing block against real Bulldog measurements and rebuilt it as a dedicated broad-chest fit | wider chest girth, shorter back length, reinforced neck binding to prevent stretching. We produced a small batch of revised samples before touching the bulk order.</p>
      
      <p><strong>Result:</strong> the brand's return rate for that SKU dropped from 18% to under 2% in the following season. The lesson generalises beyond Bulldogs | any breed with an unusual body ratio (Dachshunds, Greyhounds, barrel-chested breeds) needs its own block, not a scaled human pattern or a generic "small/medium/large" guess.</p>
      
      <p>This is why we ask for breed and body type at the design brief stage, before any pattern work starts. It costs nothing extra and prevents exactly this kind of return-rate problem. The same logic applies outside dogs | a cat harness top fitted to one breed's shoulder width will gap or bind on a Maine Coon, and a horse rug cut to a generic length will rub at the shoulders on a cob-type build. Species and body type both have to be part of the brief, not an afterthought.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

